WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:10.724
Back in the kitchen, the cook has pinned a rule to the fridge: if a diner sends a plate back, say sorry and bring the next dish.

2
00:00:10.724 --> 00:00:11.536
Good manners.

3
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:14.528
In code, the rule is a try/catch around emit.

4
00:00:14.528 --> 00:00:20.304
The diner sends a plate back by throwing an exception, and the catch says sorry and moves on.

5
00:00:20.304 --> 00:00:22.292
Tonight, the salad is too salty.

6
00:00:22.467 --> 00:00:25.406
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:25.406 --> 00:00:29.177
Catching an exception is the careful thing to do, isn't it?

8
00:00:29.177 --> 00:00:30.647
So what could go wrong?

9
00:00:30.647 --> 00:00:33.395
Soup, salad, roast: which dishes get eaten?

10
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:40.137
Here's what the lesson's TransparencyTrap prints.

11
00:00:40.137 --> 00:00:42.099
Sorry for the salad, as planned.

12
00:00:42.099 --> 00:00:44.244
Then a second sorry, for the roast.

13
00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:47.677
And collect returned normally, with just the soup eaten.

14
00:00:47.867 --> 00:00:50.685
As far as the cook knows, dinner went fine.

15
00:00:50.685 --> 00:00:52.980
The diner went home after the soup.

16
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:59.535
In a real program, that's a job that reports success with a hole in it, and a log line nobody reads.

17
00:00:59.733 --> 00:01:04.378
Lesson 1's toy flow, and the same loop in Java, serve the soup and the roast.

18
00:01:04.378 --> 00:01:07.515
So the real flow { } does something the toy doesn't.

19
00:01:07.515 --> 00:01:09.204
Something refused the roast.

20
00:01:10.733 --> 00:01:17.032
When you collect a flow { }, it first wraps your collector in a SafeCollector, and only then runs your block.

21
00:01:17.032 --> 00:01:20.731
So every emit goes to that wrapper, never straight to the diner.

22
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:24.603
Picture an inspector in the kitchen doorway, with a clipboard.

23
00:01:24.603 --> 00:01:28.038
Every plate passes the inspector on its way to the table.

24
00:01:28.038 --> 00:01:33.281
The cook is your block, the inspector is the SafeCollector, and each plate is one emit.

25
00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.693
The inspector asks three questions about every plate.

26
00:01:37.693 --> 00:01:39.275
One: is dinner still on?

27
00:01:39.275 --> 00:01:42.768
A coroutine can be cancelled, that is, asked to stop.

28
00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:47.645
If the diner's coroutine was cancelled, emit throws CancellationException.

29
00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.927
Two: is it the same kitchen, and the same cook?

30
00:01:50.927 --> 00:01:58.712
A coroutine carries a context: a few settings, such as its dispatcher, which picks the thread, its name, and its job.

31
00:01:58.712 --> 00:02:02.970
Every part but the job must be the very same as the collector's.

32
00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.299
If not: Flow invariant is violated.

33
00:02:05.467 --> 00:02:08.510
Three: did the diner send the last plate back?

34
00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:14.397
If the previous emit threw, the next one throws: Flow exception transparency is violated.

35
00:02:14.533 --> 00:02:16.462
Notice what's not on the list.

36
00:02:16.462 --> 00:02:18.776
The inspector never tastes the food.

37
00:02:18.776 --> 00:02:19.997
Strictly paperwork.

38
00:02:19.997 --> 00:02:25.397
It never looks at your values, only at who is emitting, and how the last plate went.

39
00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:28.234
Now replay the dinner.

40
00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:30.595
The soup goes through, and gets eaten.

41
00:02:30.595 --> 00:02:32.769
The salad goes out, and comes back.

42
00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:36.621
The inspector writes it down, and the polite catch says sorry.

43
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:38.704
The cook carries out the roast.

44
00:02:38.704 --> 00:02:42.511
The inspector stops her at the door: the last plate came back.

45
00:02:42.511 --> 00:02:46.932
That refusal is an exception too, and the same polite catch swallows it.

46
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:50.409
That third check has a name: exception transparency.

47
00:02:50.409 --> 00:02:55.873
A flow never hides an exception that came from downstream, and never emits after one.

48
00:02:55.873 --> 00:03:01.465
And the diner's complaint survives only as text in the message, with no cause attached.

49
00:03:02.333 --> 00:03:04.489
Not every flow has an inspector.

50
00:03:04.489 --> 00:03:14.729
The library's own operators, like map, filter, onEach and take, and builders like flowOf and asFlow, are built on an internal builder called unsafeFlow.

51
00:03:14.729 --> 00:03:18.097
It's lesson 1's toy builder, nearly word for word.

52
00:03:18.267 --> 00:03:23.224
They're family helpers who come in by the side door, and nobody checks their hands.

53
00:03:23.224 --> 00:03:28.300
The library trusts map, because your lambda in map gets a value, never the collector.

54
00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:30.211
It can't emit at the wrong time.

55
00:03:30.211 --> 00:03:33.973
transform hands you the collector, so it gets a real inspector.

56
00:03:34.133 --> 00:03:37.564
The price: skipping the inspector also skips check one.

57
00:03:37.564 --> 00:03:42.679
A collector that cancels itself and never pauses keeps getting values from asFlow.

58
00:03:42.679 --> 00:03:44.863
cancellable() puts that check back.

59
00:03:45.533 --> 00:03:48.654
And how does take(1) stop the cook after one dish?

60
00:03:48.654 --> 00:03:53.522
A flow has no line back to the kitchen, only the emit the cook is standing in.

61
00:03:53.522 --> 00:04:00.636
So take throws a note back up through emit: no more, thanks, signed with its own name, so only that take keeps it.

62
00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:03.566
Now the polite rule has a second victim.

63
00:04:03.566 --> 00:04:06.540
The catch around emit catches the note too.

64
00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:10.690
The cook says sorry, emits again, and the inspector refuses.

65
00:04:10.690 --> 00:04:14.148
Over 1,000 items, that loop logged 1,000 warnings.

66
00:04:14.148 --> 00:04:17.260
A thousand apologies for one no more, thanks.

67
00:04:18.133 --> 00:04:19.531
So the rule is simple.

68
00:04:19.531 --> 00:04:21.690
Never put a try/catch around emit.

69
00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.722
Catch where the failure happens.

70
00:04:23.722 --> 00:04:28.485
If the diner may send a plate back, catch inside the collector, around eat.

71
00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:31.610
Here's the fix in Kotlin, with Java beside it.

72
00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.450
The flow is plain again, and the sorry lives with the diner.

73
00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:37.113
The next dish still comes.

74
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:40.529
So, what could go wrong with a polite catch?

75
00:04:40.529 --> 00:04:46.519
It wraps the diner, so it swallows the diner's complaint, the inspector's refusal, and take's note.

76
00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:53.780
flow { } keeps every plate honest with three checks: dinner still on, same kitchen, and nothing after a plate came back.

77
00:04:53.933 --> 00:04:54.818
Quick check.

78
00:04:54.818 --> 00:05:00.788
A flow emits 1, then emits 2 inside withContext, with the coroutine name tasting.

79
00:05:00.788 --> 00:05:02.115
No thread changes.

80
00:05:02.115 --> 00:05:04.179
What does the collector see?

81
00:05:07.400 --> 00:05:08.105
1 arrives.

82
00:05:08.105 --> 00:05:11.491
Then collect throws: Flow invariant is violated.

83
00:05:11.491 --> 00:05:16.359
The name is part of the context, and the inspector checks every part.
